    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best grass-fed whey protein for most people is Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Isolate if you want an isolate, or Naked Whey if you want the cheapest, simplest concentrate. But before you pay the premium, know what you are buying. In the US, "grass-fed" on whey is an unregulated marketing claim; the meaningful versions are tied to Irish, New Zealand, American Grassfed Association or organic standards. And the nutritional case is thin: the protein is the same, and most of the grass-fed fat advantage is filtered out. Buy it for how the cows are raised, not for extra gains.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-grass-fed-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What "Grass-Fed" Actually Means on a Whey Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the United States, "grass-fed" on a tub of whey is a marketing claim, not a regulated standard. The USDA's Agricultural Marketing Service used to publish a voluntary grass (forage) fed marketing claim standard, and it withdrew that standard in January 2016. Nothing replaced it for dairy. So when a whey brand prints "100% grass-fed" on the front of the tub, there is no federal rulebook defining how much grass, for how many days, or who checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make every claim empty. It means the claim is only as good as the program behind it. At one end sit third-party standards with audited diets and grazing days: Ireland's Bord Bia Grass Fed Standard, the New Zealand Grass-Fed Standard, Glanbia's Truly Grass Fed program and the American Grassfed Association's dairy standard. At the other end is a brand saying its supplier's cows "graze on pasture" with no number attached and nobody auditing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the dairy cows in this debate eat a lot of grass and some grain, silage or supplementary feed. Even the strictest export standards allow some of that: the Irish and New Zealand programs both set the bar at roughly 90 percent of the diet from grass and forage, not 100. "Grass-fed" on whey almost always means "mostly grass-fed", and the honest brands say which standard they mean.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a supply-chain wrinkle that whey buyers rarely hear about. Whey is a by-product of cheese and casein making, and brands buy it from large dairy processors that pool milk from hundreds or thousands of farms. A grass-fed claim on whey is therefore a claim about a milk pool, which is why the credible standards audit the processor and set pool-level averages rather than inspecting each cow.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple. Treat "grass-fed" as a question, not an answer. The useful follow-up is: grass-fed according to whom? If the brand can name a standard and a country, the claim means something specific. If it cannot, you are paying for a word.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Grass-Fed Standards, Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is what each program behind the common whey claims requires, based on each scheme's own published materials at the time of writing. Thresholds get revised, so check the current standard if a specific number matters to you.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Standard</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Who runs it</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Diet requirement</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grazing requirement</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bord Bia Grass Fed Standard (Ireland)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Irish food board, audited via its dairy assurance scheme</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 90% grass at herd level; pooled milk averages 95%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 160 days at grass (national average about 240)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New Zealand Grass-Fed Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recognised by NZ's Ministry for Primary Industries</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 90% grass-fed feed, 3-year average</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 340 days a year, 8+ hours a day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truly Grass Fed (Glanbia, Irish milk)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ingredient supplier program, third-party audited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 95% grass, per program materials</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 250 days a year on pasture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">American Grassfed Association dairy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">US nonprofit certifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass and forage only, no grain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pasture-based; no antibiotics or added hormones</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">USDA Organic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA National Organic Program</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 30% of dry-matter intake from pasture in grazing season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 120 days a year on pasture</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The Irish and New Zealand standards are the ones you will meet most often on whey, because both countries run pasture-based dairy systems and export a great deal of whey. Ireland's Bord Bia standard audits farms through its Sustainable Dairy Assurance Scheme, tracking purchased feed and days at grass. New Zealand's national standard requires at least 90 percent grass-fed feed and grazing on at least 340 days a year; Fonterra says its own milk pool averages around 96 percent grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Truly Grass Fed is a branded program run by Glanbia, the ingredient giant behind Optimum Nutrition, using Irish milk. Legion says its Whey+ isolate is made from Truly Grass Fed certified milk, which makes it one of the few US-market whey powders that names a specific audited program on its product page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The American Grassfed Association is the strictest of the group on diet: its dairy standard is built around grass and forage only, with no grain. It is also the rarest on whey. We did not find a mainstream whey powder carrying the AGA seal at the time of writing, so treat an AGA claim on a tub as something to verify with the certifier, not assume.
          </p>

          <p>
            USDA Organic is not a grass-fed standard at all, but it is the only one of these enforced by federal regulation. Organic dairy rules require at least 120 days a year on pasture and at least 30 percent of dry-matter intake from grazing during the grazing season. That is a lower bar than the Irish or New Zealand programs, but it is a legal floor with inspections behind it. Our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide covers that side in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Grass-Fed Whey Protein Powders
