        <div id="nutrition" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Grass-Fed Whey Change the Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Honestly, not by much, and the evidence that it matters for a protein powder is thin. The best-documented difference between grass-fed and conventional milk is in the fat. A 2018 study by Benbrook and colleagues in Food Science &amp; Nutrition found that milk from 100 percent grass-fed cows had an omega-6 to omega-3 ratio close to 1:1, against nearly 6:1 for conventional milk, along with more conjugated linoleic acid (CLA).
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is that whey protein is made by removing most of the fat. A whey concentrate like Naked Whey or Levels carries about 1.5 to 2.5 g of total fat per serving, per the labels at the time of writing. A whey isolate like Transparent Labs carries around 1 g or less. Whatever omega-3 or CLA advantage the milk had, a scoop delivers a tiny fraction of it. You would get more omega-3 from a few bites of salmon than from a year of grass-fed whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein itself is essentially the same molecule. Whey's amino acid profile, including the roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine per 25 g serving that drives muscle protein synthesis, is set by the proteins in milk, not by the cow's diet. Naked Whey lists about 3 g of leucine per 25 g serving and Levels about 2.5 g per 24 g serving, per their amino profiles, which is in line with conventional whey. We found no controlled trial showing grass-fed whey builds more muscle than conventional whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where grass-fed can make a real difference is in what comes with it. Many grass-fed standards, and the dairy systems in Ireland and New Zealand, exclude or simply do not use recombinant bovine growth hormone, and some restrict antibiotics. Those are welfare and farming-practice differences more than nutrition differences, and they are legitimate reasons to choose a product. They are just not reasons to expect better gains.
          </p>

          <p>
            So buy grass-fed whey because you care about how the cows are raised, or because the grass-fed brands also happen to run shorter labels and better testing. Do not buy it expecting a measurable health or performance edge from the protein itself. If a brand claims one, ask for the study.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hormones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. rBGH-Free, Hormone-Free and the Other Claims Next to Grass-Fed
          </h2>

          <p>
            "rBGH-free" or "rBST-free" is the claim most often printed alongside grass-fed, and it is a separate thing. Recombinant bovine somatotropin is a synthetic growth hormone that the FDA approved for US dairy cows in 1993 to raise milk yield. It is not authorised for use in the European Union, Canada or New Zealand, so whey made from Irish, European or New Zealand milk is rBST-free by default, grass-fed or not.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the US, use of rBST has fallen sharply since its peak as processors and retailers dropped it, and the FDA's position is that it has found no significant difference between milk from treated and untreated cows. That is why you will often see a qualifying note on rBST-free labels. The claim is about farming practice, not about a hormone you could detect in the scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            "Hormone-free" is looser language. All milk contains naturally occurring hormones, so no whey is literally hormone-free. When a brand like Transparent Labs or Levels says hormone-free, read it as "no added growth hormones were used on the cows". That is a reasonable claim, but it is a supplier statement rather than a certification unless the brand names who verified it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isopure is a useful example of how these claims separate. Its Zero Carb and Low Carb isolates are labelled rBST-free whey but make no grass-fed claim, per the label at the time of writing. If avoiding added hormones is your real priority, an rBST-free conventional whey may get you there for less money than a grass-fed tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Non-GMO is the other common companion claim. On dairy it usually refers to the cows' feed. It tells you nothing about grazing, and grass-fed cows on a pasture-based diet eat relatively little of the GMO corn and soy the claim is aimed at anyway. The claims overlap, but none of them implies the others.
          </p>
        </div>

