        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming grass-fed is a regulated term</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In the US, no federal standard defines grass-fed dairy since the USDA withdrew its voluntary claim standard in 2016. A front-of-tub claim can mean anything from audited Irish pasture milk to a supplier's loose assurance. Look for a named standard, such as Bord Bia, the New Zealand standard, Truly Grass Fed or AGA, before paying extra.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting grass-fed whey to build more muscle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The amino acids in whey, including leucine, come from milk proteins, not the cow's diet. No controlled trial we found shows grass-fed whey builds more muscle than conventional whey. The documented grass-fed advantage is in milk fat, and whey removes most of the fat, so the difference per scoop is tiny.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing rBST-free with grass-fed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                rBST-free means the cows were not given synthetic growth hormone. It says nothing about grazing. Irish, European and New Zealand whey is rBST-free by default because the hormone is not authorised there, and some US conventional wheys carry the claim too. If added hormones are your only concern, you may not need grass-fed at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a reseller's grass-fed description</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Third-party listings and review sites sometimes call a whey grass-fed when the brand does not. Ascent is the clearest case: some reviews describe it as grass-fed, but the brand's own support FAQ says its cows are not grass-fed. Check the manufacturer's wording, ideally on the label and product page, before you buy for that reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grass-fed wheys range from 20 g to 28 g of protein per scoop and come in 1 lb to 5 lb sizes, so the sticker price misleads. Divide price by servings, divide by protein grams, then multiply by 25. On our checks the real spread ran from about $1.84 to $3.50 per 25 g, nearly double.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

